# The Registration Form Is the Pledge Card

Tish Brooks runs the 5K.

Latisha "Tish" Brooks has been the community events manager at a pediatric hospital foundation in Charlotte, North Carolina, for nine years. Her desk is on the second floor of a low glass building on East Fourth Street, in a small windowless office she shares with a whiteboard she has not erased since 2019 and a Keurig she has not descaled since 2022. There is a framed bib on the wall behind her monitor — number 001, from the very first Miracle Miles 5K in the fall of 2017 — and, in Sharpie across the top, the words *Marley — thank you.* Marley is eight now. Tish keeps every birthday card the family has ever sent her in a shoebox in her bottom drawer.

Every January 15 at eight a.m., registration opens for the Miracle Miles 5K, the foundation's largest peer-to-peer event. Tish sits at her desk with a fresh coffee and a dashboard that autorefreshes every thirty seconds. In the first hour, thirty to sixty teams typically register. Most are family teams under ten people, minimum donation $50. A handful are corporate teams from the two big banks uptown, minimum $100. A few are hospital-staff teams from the peds neuro-ICU or the hem-onc floor, no minimum, capped at fifty out of respect for the shift schedule.

At 8:47 a.m. on a Thursday morning in the third week of January, a team registration came in that did not fit any of those shapes.

*Team Marley — 40 members, minimum donation $5,000, captain: Deb Aizawa.*

Tish opened the registration at 8:48. She read the paragraph Deb had typed into the *Tell us about your team* free-text field:

*Fourth year running. Marley was in the peds neuro-ICU for eleven days in February 2023. She is in third grade now and wants to be a nurse. My employer — Coyle-Hendrickson Asset Management, uptown — has agreed to match every dollar our team raises up to $250,000, and I have committed personally to a $5,000 floor for anyone who wants to run with us. If a friend of a friend of a friend wants to sign up, put them on the list. Marley wants to hand out the medals at the finish line.*

By 8:52, Tish had cross-referenced Deb's registration to three prior years of team data. Team Marley had registered twelve people in 2024 and raised $8,400. Twenty-two people in 2025 and raised $19,200. Deb had personally given $2,500, $5,000, and $10,000 across those three years — plus a $50 monthly recurring gift she set up in March 2023 the week Marley came home, which the CRM keys under *sustainer* and no major-gifts officer has ever opened.

By 9:15, Tish had done something the events-platform vendor's help documentation did not describe. She had opened the primary-donor record for the account (*Aizawa, Deborah H.*) and searched for the last time anyone with the word *development* on their door had touched the file.

The most recent contact note was March 2023. It read: *Sent a plush lion to Marley's room, per Nurse Maria's request. Family declined a meeting — mom said "not yet."*

Three years. No follow-up. No coffee. No thank-you note from the president. Deb had, in the meantime, become the seventeenth-largest peer-to-peer captain the foundation had, was about to become the third-largest, and — through Coyle-Hendrickson — was about to unlock a corporate match the foundation's corporate-giving team had been trying, and failing, to get a meeting for since 2022.

Tish had never been asked what she was seeing.

## What Tish knew by 9:15

Tish knew Deb Aizawa is a managing director at Coyle-Hendrickson Asset Management. She knew — from a LinkedIn skim the morning Deb first registered a team in 2024 — that Deb had joined the Charlotte office in 2018 and had, in 2022, been promoted to run the Southeast institutional practice. She knew Coyle-Hendrickson's own foundation gave $6.4M to health causes last year, and that the firm's giving decisions run through the same institutional-practice desk Deb now sits at.

She knew, because she had watched the shape twice this year already, that a Team-Marley-scale registration on the first Thursday of the season meant Deb had asked her firm for the corporate match on a phone call sometime in the quiet week between Christmas and New Year's. She knew that ask does not get made unless the person asking has already decided, privately, that this is her institution.

She knew what nobody with the word *development* on their door would know for six weeks.

Tish read Deb's paragraph twice. Then she picked up the phone.

## What the CRM will show in early March

The CRM will show, by the first Monday in March, that the Miracle Miles 5K registered 4,127 walkers and runners across 218 teams and raised, before the event even happened, $487,000 in peer-to-peer commitments.

It will show a $5,000 registration fee from Deborah H. Aizawa under *event revenue.* It will show the team totals as they come in. On the Sunday after the event, it will show a $137,000 total for Team Marley — plus a $137,000 matching wire from Coyle-Hendrickson the following Tuesday.

It will not show that Deb was one phone call away from an ongoing conversation about the foundation's planned capital campaign for a new pediatric neuro-oncology unit. It will not show that her firm's Charlotte office had, in December, added the foundation to the internal shortlist of local nonprofits they were vetting as an anchor community partner for 2027. It will not show that Marley wants to be a nurse.

The CRM will store a total.

It will not store the paragraph.

## The registration form is the pledge card

The team registration for a peer-to-peer 5K is a web form with about a dozen fields. Team name. Captain name. Captain email. Team size cap. Minimum donation. Company affiliation. Shirt sizes. And — down at the bottom, above the *Submit* button — a free-text box labeled *Tell us about your team.*

It is the only field on the registration where the donor speaks in her own voice.

Everything else — the payment processor, the confirmation email, the widget that spins up the team's fundraising page — is required by the event platform. The free-text box is required by nobody. It is the captain's editorial choice. It is where she tells you *why.*

*In memory of Miles, our brother, our best friend, our teammate.*

*Because Nurse Denise stayed the whole night with Amelia in December.*

*For the neurosurgery fellows, from the family of a girl they saved in 2019.*

*Team Marley. Fourth year. My employer will match up to $250K.*

The box will accept five hundred characters. Most captains use thirty. A few use all five hundred. The paragraph Deb Aizawa wrote at 8:47 on a Thursday morning in January is a $487,000 pledge card and a corporate-partnership introduction and a warm handoff to a major-gifts officer who has never met the family. It is filed, on most peer-to-peer platforms, in an unstructured JSON field the CRM syncs once a day into a *Notes* column nobody on the second floor reads.

The registration is confirmed. The team page goes live. The captain receives a form email thanking her for her *generous participation in this year's event.*

The five hundred characters that told the whole story sit in a text field the events manager reads and the development office does not.

## What Rōmy does with the free-text box

Rōmy sits inside event registration.

When Deb submits the Team Marley registration at 8:47, Rōmy reads the free-text field the same morning. It parses the corporate-match commitment. It cross-references *Coyle-Hendrickson Asset Management* against the foundation's institutional-giving pipeline and against publicly filed 990s across the Southeast. It matches Deb's name to the CRM record for *Aizawa, Deborah H.,* pulls her three-year peer-to-peer history, and lines the shape of the ask up against the shape of the last two comparable captains — one of whom, in 2024, quietly became a $500,000 principal-gifts donor within eighteen months.

By 10:04 a.m., the director of major gifts has a note in her inbox:

*This morning's Team Marley registration is the largest first-time peer-to-peer captain commitment the foundation has ever received from a prospect not already in the major-gifts pipeline. Deb Aizawa is a managing director at Coyle-Hendrickson (Southeast institutional-practice head since 2022). Her firm's foundation gave $6.4M to health causes last year and has us on a 2027 anchor-partner shortlist per an internal source. Her daughter Marley is eight, in third grade at Providence Day, wants to be a nurse. Suggested: a personal note from you to Deb this week, in your own voice — thanking her for the fourth-year commitment, congratulating her on running the Southeast desk, and offering, at her convenience in the spring, thirty minutes of the president's time and a private tour of the peds neuro floor with Marley if she'd like to bring her along. Do not mention the campaign. Not yet.*

The director sends the note on Friday morning before her nine a.m.

By March, Deb has met the president over lunch at a place near her office uptown. By June, Marley is standing in a small stiff dress at the ribbon-cutting for the pediatric neurosurgery family lounge, funded by a $2.5M gift from the Coyle-Hendrickson Foundation and a $500,000 personal top-up from Deb and her wife. By December, the foundation has an anchor-partner memorandum on Coyle-Hendrickson letterhead and a soft invitation to name a neurosurgery fellowship in the memory of Deb's uncle, who died of a glioma in 2001.

The window that would have closed by the second Monday in March — the window during which the foundation could still say, in one sentence Deb would remember, *we saw you* — stayed open.

## The takeaway you can act on Monday

Walk down the hall on Monday morning.

The person running your peer-to-peer events is doing more prospect research, per week, than most people on your major-gifts team. She reads the *Tell us about your team* box five hundred characters at a time. In each one, she knows something you do not, and — this is the part your director will not like — she has never been told her noticing is worth a promotion.

Take her to coffee. Take her to lunch. Ask her which team captains, this year, are running for a reason.

Then listen.

You do not need a new events platform. You do not need a bigger 5K. You need the plumbing that carries what Tish reads at 8:47 on a Thursday morning to the desk of the person who can send Deb Aizawa a two-line note before her Friday-afternoon meeting with the head of her firm's foundation.

The registration form is the pledge card.

Tish has been reading it, five hundred characters at a time, since 2017.

Nobody has told her she is running major gifts.

She is.
